# November 22, 2026: a second scatter of places across Alberta, the province with the fewest places on the map
# (32 before this batch). Region ab taken with tools/choose.py --claim --thinnest. Spread from the Peace River country
# (Fort Vermilion, Grouard) through the parkland (Ponoka, Red Deer, Markerville,
# Flagstaff County, Rowley) to the foothills (Turner Valley), the mountains (Canmore, Banff, Jasper, Waterton) and the
# south (Lethbridge, Cardston). Sources opened in this session: the Canadian Register of Historic Places statement for
# each place (historicplaces.ca, read whole; the register ids come from the {{HPC row}} templates of Wikipedia's
# Alberta historic-place lists); Wikipedia as raw text; news reports (CBC, the Catholic Register, the Western Wheel);
# and the page of whoever runs each place for hours (Parks Canada, the Galt Museum, the Cardston & District Historical
# Society, Historic Markerville, Rowley's own site, the Glacier Park Collection, the Government of Alberta's 2024
# historic-sites brochure).
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'ab_yknm','label':'Alberta from the Peace to Waterton: elevators, creameries, courthouses and a gas plant','added':'2026-11-22 11:51'}
PLACES=[
# ---- The Peace River country ----
N('hbc-factors-house-fort-vermilion','Hudson\'s Bay Company Factor\'s House','Fort Vermilion','ab','landmark','standing',58.3966,-116.002,'site','view','drive','','1906–1908',
  'The North West Company opened a post on the Peace River flats here in 1788, and the Hudson\'s Bay Company built a provisions post in 1830 that supplied bison and moose meat to the boat brigades and to Fort Chipewyan and Dunvegan; it was the company\'s chief post on the Peace until 1878. This house was built in 1906 to 1908, of timber sawn at the company\'s own Fort Vermilion mill, and the company\'s factors lived in it from 1908 until 1940. The Register calls it the only structural remnant of the fur trade there, and the only one of Alberta\'s three surviving factors\' houses still on its original site.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey balloon-frame house on a T-shaped plan: steep cross-gabled shingle roof with brackets, bay windows with hipped roofs on the south and east sides, half-round windows in the gables, two corbelled masonry chimneys, V-joint siding, and what remains of an L-shaped verandah with a balcony above on the north side. The Register lists the original newel posts and staircase inside. Whether the public can go in was not checked; look from the street.',
  'The fur-trade boss\'s house on the Peace, the last of the old post.',
  'A Provincial Historic Resource on its original lot in Fort Vermilion, overlooking the Peace River. Who owns it now and whether it opens to visitors were not checked: treat it as look-only from the public road. Fort Vermilion is on Highway 88, far north of Peace River.',
  [HP('Hudson\'s Bay Company Factor\'s House',5723)],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('st-bernard-mission-grouard','St. Bernard Mission: the bishops\' cemetery and the burned church','Grouard','ab','worship','ruins',55.5447,-116.158,'area','view','drive','burned','1872–2023',
  'The Missionary Oblates of Mary Immaculate founded St. Bernard Mission in 1872 near the Hudson\'s Bay post at Lesser Slave Lake, and by 1900 it was one of the largest missions in northern Alberta, with a church, rectory, nuns\' residence, boarding school and farm. Bishop Emile Grouard made it his seat from about 1901 until his death in 1931, and the town took his name. Brother Augustin Dumas built the church in 1902 as Grouard\'s cathedral, and the bishop painted the crucifixion behind the altar himself. On May 22, 2023 the church was set on fire; the walls were left standing but the archbishop called it irreparably destroyed, and in June 2025 the parish was saying Mass in the Kapawe\'no First Nation school and hoped to rebuild.',
  'The cemetery north of the church site, in use since at least 1873, holds four Catholic bishops, Grouard among them, and many missionaries, with stone markers and wooden fencing and views to Buffalo Bay. Whether the burned shell of the church still stands, or has been cleared or rebuilt, was not checked after June 2025.',
  'The Artist Bishop\'s cathedral, lost to arson in 2023, and the graves beside it.',
  'In the hamlet of Grouard, Big Lakes County, 13 km north of Highway 2, on mission land of the Archdiocese of Grouard-McLennan. Whether the cemetery is open to walk in was not checked: look from the road unless the parish says otherwise. The pin is the Register\'s position for the mission.',
  [HP('St. Bernard Mission (Church and Cemetery)',9089),W('Grouard'),L('CBC News: fire rips through historic church (2023)','https://www.cbc.ca/lite/story/1.6852531'),L('The Catholic Register: church arsonist fails to appear (2025)','https://www.catholicregister.org/item/2364-church-arsonist-fails-to-appear-for-sentencing')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
# ---- The parkland ----
N('diplomat-mine-forestburg','Diplomat Mine strip-mining machines','Forestburg','ab','industry','standing',52.5055,-112.191,'site','view','drive','','1927–1986',
  'The Bish family, homesteaders from Oregon, opened underground coal mines here in 1907 and worked them until 1949; Luscar Coals took over in 1950 and strip-mined the ground until 1986. The Marion 360 shovel, built in 1927 and once the largest mobile land machine in the world, came here from a mine in Illinois in 1950. A Bucyrus 950-B of 1937, "Mr. Diplomat", the largest excavator in the world when it was built, came in 1962, and a smaller Bucyrus-Erie 120-B of 1944 in 1964. After the mine closed, much of the land was reclaimed for farming.',
  'Two huge strip-mining machines, the riveted Marion 360 (27 m boom) and the Bucyrus-Erie 120-B, and the 25-cubic-metre scoop of the Bucyrus 950-B, the "Big Dipper", which is all that is left of it, on 2.5 hectares of reclaimed mine, with spoil piles in sight to the south-west. The Government of Alberta\'s energy-history pages call it an interpretive site and Canada\'s only surface coal-mining museum.',
  'Strip-mining giants, one of them once the biggest land machine on earth.',
  'A Provincial Historic Resource about 15 km south-west of Forestburg, Flagstaff County. OpenStreetMap maps a parking lot off Highway 855 beside it (used here). The page of whoever runs the interpretive site could not be opened when this was written, so its hours and rules were not checked: look from the lot until you know more.',
  [HP('Diplomat Mine Site',7985),L('Alberta energy history: the Marion 360 stripping shovel','https://history.alberta.ca/energyheritage/coal/reinvention-1950-onwards/transformation-and-innovation/marion-360-stripping-shovel-excavator.aspx')],
  th=[52.50589,-112.19074],thn='Lot off Highway 855',brick='no'),
N('alberta-hospital-ponoka','Alberta Hospital Building No. 1','Ponoka','ab','inst','standing',52.6507,-113.574,'area','view','drive','','1908–1912',
  'Before Alberta had an asylum of its own, its patients were sent to Brandon, Manitoba. The province began this one in 1908 on a rural site south-east of Ponoka, chosen because fresh air and nature were then thought to heal; it opened in 1911 as the Alberta Hospital for the Insane, and building went on to 1912. Later called the Provincial Mental Health Hospital, it was the province\'s main mental hospital through the first half of the century. The provincial architect A. M. Jeffers, who also designed the Legislature, based it on the State Lunatic Asylum at Utica, New York.',
  'A three-storey red brick building in the Classical Revival style with a rock-faced sandstone basement, sandstone string courses, a low hipped roof with cupolas and corbelled chimneys, a heavy bracketed cornice, and a columned front verandah carrying a balcony. Most of the Utica-style porches are gone. Inside, the Register lists an old pharmacy of wood-panelled walls and riveted steel stairs. It is still the centre of a working mental-health and brain-injury hospital: look from the grounds\' public roads.',
  'Alberta\'s first asylum, copied from Utica.',
  'A working provincial hospital campus on about 65 hectares on the south-east edge of Ponoka. Not open to sightseers: look from the public way. The pin is the Register\'s position for the campus.',
  [HP('Alberta Hospital Building No. 1',5894)],
  brick='yes'),
N('old-red-deer-courthouse','Old Red Deer Courthouse','Red Deer','ab','civic','standing',52.26932,-113.81117,'exact','view','drive','','1931',
  'Planned as early as 1907 but not built until 1931, under the United Farmers government\'s policy of spreading public buildings beyond Calgary and Edmonton. The Register calls it the last Alberta courthouse built in the Classical Revival style. It housed the provincial court and the Court of Queen\'s Bench until 1983, with a rear addition of 1958, and in 1959 it saw the murder trial of Robert Raymond Cooke, the last person sentenced to hang in Alberta. Since then it has served as an arts centre, offices and shops.',
  'A two-storey building of red brick in Flemish bond with Tyndall limestone trim brought from Manitoba and a terracotta tile roof. The main entry has a two-storey Ionic portico, an arched door with a fanlight, and a pediment with a carved provincial crest. Inside, the Register lists the vaulted second-floor courtroom, the judges\' chambers and jury room, a rotunda and birch woodwork, and the City of Red Deer a terrazzo staircase. Whether the interior can be seen depends on the businesses in it; not checked.',
  'Alberta\'s last classical courthouse, where the province\'s last death sentence was passed.',
  'Privately used building at 4836 Ross Street, on the corner of 49 Avenue in downtown Red Deer. Look from the street; street parking downtown. A Provincial Historic Resource since 1986.',
  [HP('Old Red Deer Courthouse',3445),L('City of Red Deer: Old Court House','https://www.reddeer.ca/about-red-deer/history/heritage/community-heritage-planning/red-deers-inventory-of-heritage-sites/downtown-heritage-sites-gallery/cul---old-court-house---heritage-site.html')],
  brick='yes'),
N('markerville-creamery','Markerville Creamery','Markerville','ab','industry','standing',52.12352,-114.17223,'exact','open','drive','','1902–1972',
  'Settlers west of Innisfail formed the Tindastoll Butter and Cheese Manufacturer\'s Association in 1899 and opened one of the first creameries under the North-West Territories dairy board in Markerville, an Icelandic settlement named after the dairy commissioner C. P. Marker. The Danish cheesemaker Dan Morkeberg ran it from 1899 until 1924, and with no railway the village grew on the creamery\'s success. This building replaced the first, smaller one in 1902; an ice house followed in 1940. The creamery made butter for more than seven decades and closed in 1972.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey wood frame creamery restored to its 1932 look: cedar-shingled roof with a cross gable and two ventilation cupolas, a tall corbelled chimney, grey siding with red trim and a verandah round two sides, with some of the old interior and the creamery\'s machinery inside; and the plain 1940 ice house. Tours of the butter creamery, and a café (the Kaffistofa) serving coffee, light lunches and ice cream.',
  'A 1902 co-op butter creamery in an Icelandic hamlet.',
  'Run as a museum by the Stephan G. Stephansson Icelandic Society (Historic Markerville) at 114 Creamery Way, Markerville, Red Deer County. Admission charged (adults $5 on its page). Its 2026 season closed September 7. Hours from the society\'s creamery page.',
  [HP('The Markerville Creamery',8453),L('Historic Markerville: Markerville Creamery Museum','https://www.historicmarkerville.com/markerville-creamery-museum.html')],
  brick='no',hours='May to June: Wednesday to Sunday and holiday Mondays 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; July and August: daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed fall and winter'),
N('rowley-elevator-row','Rowley grain elevator row','Rowley','ab','town','standing',51.76134,-112.78814,'site','view','drive','','1917–1989',
  'The Alberta Midland Railway reached this spot about 25 km north of Drumheller in 1911, and the townsite of Rowley grew up to serve the farms round it. Its elevators went up in the boom years of the First World War and after: a United Grain Growers elevator in 1917 (the only one of that company\'s left in Alberta), bought by the Alberta Wheat Pool in 1928 and twinned in 1940, and a Searle Grain Company elevator in 1923, the oldest Searle elevator still standing in the province. Freight service between Rowley and Morrin ended in 1989 and the elevators closed. The Register calls the row one of the finest left in Alberta; the hamlet keeps up the old main street around it.',
  'Wooden crib elevators with windowless shafts and cupolas, the Alberta Wheat Pool and Searle names still painted on, with their offices, engine shed and fuel shed beside the old rail bed. The Register lists the original belts and cups, rope man-lifts, Gerber wheels and scales still inside. Rowley\'s summer tours show the outside of the elevators (no one may go in), the school museum, station, trading post, garage and the bank and funeral home used as sets for the 1989 film Bye Bye Blues.',
  'A ghost-town street with one of Alberta\'s best rows of wooden elevators.',
  'In the hamlet of Rowley, Starland County, north of Drumheller. Look from the street any time; the elevators may not be entered. Free guided tours (tips welcome) in summer: in 2026 from June 27 to August 22, Thursday to Monday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., last tour at 4 p.m., by Rowley\'s own site. Some buildings, among them Sam\'s Saloon and the old hospital, are private: stay off private property.',
  [HP('Searle Grain Company Grain Elevator Site Complex',8983),HP('United Grain Growers - Alberta Wheat Pool Grain Elevator Site Complex',8982),L('Rowley, Alberta: tours','https://www.rowleyalberta.ca/visit/tours')],
  brick='no'),
# ---- Foothills and mountains ----
N('turner-valley-gas-plant','Turner Valley Gas Plant','Turner Valley','ab','industry','standing',50.67156,-114.27017,'site','open','drive','','1913–1985',
  'Calgary Petroleum Products struck natural gas and naphtha at Dingman No. 1 on May 14, 1914, the first major gas find in Canada west of Ontario and the start of Alberta\'s petroleum industry. The company built Canada\'s first compressor and absorption plant here; after a fire in 1920 the works passed to Royalite, part of Imperial Oil, which added the first sour-gas scrubbing plant in Canada (1925), a propane plant and a sulphur plant (1952). Waste gas flared off the field led the province to set up the Turner Valley Conservation Board, forerunner of its energy regulators. The plant closed in 1985 and is a national and provincial historic site.',
  'An almost complete gas works spread over about 150 acres on the north bank of the Sheep River: the scrubbing plant, compressors and well sites, storage tanks, sheds, warehouses and offices, mostly concrete and steel built between 1930 and the late 1950s and spaced well apart to stop another fire. Some structures have been taken down for safety. There are exhibits and guided tours through some of the buildings; an interpretive centre is in the old light plant.',
  'Where Alberta\'s oil and gas industry began.',
  'A Government of Alberta historic site on Sunset Boulevard SE, Diamond Valley (the Register gives it as the Town of Turner Valley). Open seasonally, May long weekend to Labour Day, by the province\'s 2024 historic-sites brochure; the site\'s own hours page could not be opened when this was written, so days and times were not checked. Admission charged. Where visitors park was not checked on the aerial.',
  [HP('Turner Valley Gas Works',15532),L('Government of Alberta: Experience Alberta\'s history (2024 brochure)','https://alberta.ca/system/files/acsw-experience-albertas-history-brochure-2024.pdf'),L('Western Wheel: gas plant opening another season (2018)','https://www.westernwheel.ca/wheels-west/gas-plant-opening-another-season-1536010')],
  brick='?',hours='May long weekend to Labour Day (days and times not checked)'),
N('canmore-nwmp-barracks','Canmore North West Mounted Police Barracks','Canmore','ab','civic','standing',51.08903,-115.35648,'exact','open','drive','','1893',
  'The president of the Canadian Anthracite Coal Company asked for more police in 1888 to stop liquor being smuggled in to the miners and railway workers. One constable lived in rented rooms until these barracks were built in 1893. A one- or two-man detachment held them until 1929, patrolling, searching trains for liquor and keeping order during strikes, the 1911 strike above all. The building was taken down and put back up in 1908 and 1909 and enlarged in 1920 to 1923. The Register calls it one of only three Mounted Police buildings in Alberta left from before 1905.',
  'A one-storey log building with compound dovetail corners, mud-and-straw chinking and a whitewashed finish, a front verandah and two shed-roofed additions, beside Policeman\'s Creek at the east end of Canmore\'s main street. Spruce trees from the time it was built still stand round it, and whitewashed stones mark the paths as they once did. The Register lists the original floors, interior doors and wall finishes inside.',
  'The Mounties\' log post in a coal town.',
  'At 609 8 Street, Canmore. Run by the Canmore Museum (whose main museum is in the Civic Centre at 902B 7th Avenue); its barracks page could not be opened when this was written, so hours were not checked. Paid street parking nearby, by the museum\'s page.',
  [HP('Canmore North West Mounted Police Barracks',4999),L('Canmore Museum','https://canmoremuseum.com/canmore-museum/')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('cave-and-basin-banff','Cave and Basin','Banff','ab','water','standing',51.1691,-115.59117,'site','open','walk','','1883–1914',
  'Warm mineral springs rise from Sulphur Mountain into a cave and an open basin here, long known to Indigenous people. The Register says non-Indigenous people found them in 1859. They and 16 square kilometres round them were reserved for public use in 1885, and by 1887 the reserve had grown into Canada\'s first national park, the Rocky Mountain Park. Beside the vent hole stood a log shanty called "the Hotel", of 1883 to 1885, of which traces remain. The stone bathing pavilion with its long hot-spring pool was the first big work of the new National Parks Branch, set up in 1911, and was built in 1912 to 1914.',
  'The cave with its pool and the original vent hole in the roof, the open-air basin, and the rough grey Rundle limestone pavilion with two octagonal red-tile-roofed lookouts, an arcaded stone wall with iron lanterns, rooftop terraces, and a wooden bathhouse rebuilt to its 1904 form. Exhibits inside; trails lead out from the site.',
  'The hot springs where Canada\'s national parks began.',
  'Run by Parks Canada at 311 Cave Avenue, at the west end of Banff, inside Banff National Park (a park pass is needed). Admission charged. Parking at the site. Hours from Parks Canada\'s hours page; it also lists a few closed days each season.',
  [HP('Cave and Basin National Historic Site of Canada',7567),HP('Cave and Basin Bathing Pavilion',3088),W('Cave and Basin National Historic Site'),L('Parks Canada: Cave and Basin hours','https://www.parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/ab/caveandbasin/visit/heures-hours')],
  brick='no',hours='May 15 to October 15: daily 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m.; October 16 to May 14: Thursday to Monday 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed December 25'),
N('jasper-cnr-station','Jasper railway station','Jasper','ab','rail','standing',52.87618,-118.07962,'exact','view','drive','stations','1925',
  'The Canadian National Railways built this station in 1925 (the Register\'s date), a few years after the company was formed in 1919, one of the few large stations it built then, on the site of the original station. It was the dramatic entrance to Jasper Park for the tourists of the new resort era, and the Register calls it an early example of a station built to serve luxury resorts. It is a Classified Federal Heritage Building.',
  'A one-storey Arts and Crafts station with a steep roof framed by tall pointed gables, a shed dormer, a canopy over the tracks and, on the town side, the roof swept down to form a carriage porch. Off-white stucco over brick, cobblestone facing on the base and the chimney, a limestone drip course, and multi-pane windows. Interior not checked.',
  'The mountain station that welcomed Jasper\'s first tourists.',
  'On Connaught Drive at the foot of Elm Avenue in the town of Jasper, inside Jasper National Park (a park pass is needed). Look from the street and the platform areas open to the public. Its current use was not checked. The pin is OpenStreetMap\'s station point.',
  [HP('Canadian National Railway (CNR) Station, Jasper',3090)],
  brick='no'),
# ---- The south ----
N('prince-of-wales-hotel-waterton','Prince of Wales Hotel','Waterton Park','ab','hotel','standing',49.05845,-113.90407,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1926–1927',
  'The Great Northern Railway built this hotel in 1926 and 1927, its only Canadian link in a chain of park resorts across the line in Montana, in the Swiss-chalet style of its other hotels. A large hotel was thought vital to making Waterton Lakes National Park a destination. It was named a national historic site in 1992 for its rustic design and its place in national-park tourism, and it still takes guests.',
  'A blocky six-storey wooden hotel on a ridge above Upper Waterton Lake: steep intersecting gables, two-storey dormers, tiers of balconies on big brackets and a lantern cupola, in contrasting colours. Inside, a timber-framed lobby open to the roof ridge with natural wood finishes and the original doors and windows, tall windows over the lake, and a historic elevator. Afternoon tea is served in the lobby lounge.',
  'A railway chalet on a ridge above Waterton Lake.',
  'A working hotel run by the Glacier Park Collection inside Waterton Lakes National Park (a park pass is needed). Open May to October by the operator\'s page; the exact opening and closing days change each year and were not checked.',
  [HP('Prince of Wales Hotel National Historic Site of Canada',4307),W('Prince of Wales Hotel'),L('Glacier Park Collection: Prince of Wales Hotel','https://www.glacierparkcollection.com/lodging/prince-of-wales-hotel/')],
  brick='no',hours='May to October (exact dates not checked)'),
N('galt-hospital-lethbridge','Sir Alexander Galt Hospital (Galt Museum)','Lethbridge','ab','inst','standing',49.69243,-112.8473,'site','open','drive','','1908–1955',
  'The Galt family, founders of the Alberta Railway and Coal Company, paid for a first hospital on this site in 1891. In 1907, a year after a nine-month miners\' strike, the Galt family gave $30,000 for a bigger one, which the city matched; built in 1908 to 1910, it was opened by Prime Minister Wilfrid Laurier and took 65 patients. It was essentially a company hospital for the coal miners and served the town too, until 1955. It is the only building left in Lethbridge tied to the Galt family, and is now the Galt Museum & Archives.',
  'A two-storey Edwardian Classical building of brick on a sandstone foundation, with pilasters, a parapet with balusters, a cornice and limestone sills and keystones, facing down 5th Street over the Oldman River valley. Stabilised remains of part of the earlier hospital are kept on the site. Inside, museum galleries; the old interior was not checked.',
  'The coal company\'s hospital, opened by Laurier, now the city museum.',
  'The Galt Museum & Archives, 502 1 Street South, Lethbridge, at the west end of downtown. Admission charged ($9.95 plus tax for ages 6 and up on its page). Closed Christmas Eve, Christmas, Boxing Day, New Year\'s Day and Easter Sunday; holidays 1 to 5 p.m. Hours from the museum\'s visit page.',
  [HP('Sir Alexander Galt Hospital',11623),L('Galt Museum & Archives: visit','https://galtmuseum.com/visit')],
  brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sunday 1 to 5 p.m.; closed Monday'),
N('cardston-courthouse','Cardston Courthouse','Cardston','ab','civic','standing',49.19932,-113.30431,'site','open','drive','','1906–1979',
  'The first courthouse built by the new Province of Alberta after 1905, put up between 1906 and 1909 of sandstone quarried by the St. Mary River and manufactured brick. It held a courtroom, chambers, offices and jury rooms upstairs, and a North West Mounted Police detachment with an apartment and cells in the basement. It was used as a courthouse from 1907 until 1979, longer than any other in Alberta, and is the only provincial court building in the Richardsonian Romanesque style.',
  'A one-storey flat-roofed building of rusticated sandstone and brick with big arched windows, a parapet and a heavy dentilled cornice. Inside, the vaulted courtroom with the judge\'s bench and witness stand, wood wainscoting, and the original jail cells, graffiti and all. Now the Courthouse & Heritage Museum, with local history and displays on the Kainai (Blackfoot) people.',
  'Alberta\'s first provincial courthouse, cells and graffiti included.',
  'The Courthouse & Heritage Museum of the Cardston & District Historical Society, 89 3 Avenue West, Cardston. Admission charged (adults $5, which also covers the Card Pioneer Home within 24 hours). Group tours can be booked out of season. The society\'s page gives the summer hours under a 2024 heading.',
  [HP('Cardston Courthouse',3051),L('Cardston & District Historical Society: our museums','https://cardstonhistoricalsociety.org/visit')],
  brick='?',hours='May to August: 9 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. (days not given)'),
]
